{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_slbev7deyokrwuquamwd6bsh3yd2b7o3svrybbnbypmfkskeubwq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope enumeration\n\nI built the C library first and enumerated its full export surface rather than assuming:\n\n- `c_src/` contains exactly **3 files**: `CMakeLists.txt`, `include/lib.h`, `src/lib.c` (48 lines total). The CMake glob compiles only `src/lib.c`.\n- `nm -D --defined-only` on the C `.so` yields exactly **one** public symbol: `bin2hex`. The header declares no others, and there are no namespace-renaming macros (no `#define foo NAMESPACE(foo)` pattern), so the linker symbol is the plain source name.\n\nThis is a genuinely small library — so the whole thing is translated, not a corner of it. I did the work directly rather than delegating, since a 48-line surface didn't warrant sub-agents.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n`comm -23` of the two symbol lists is **empty** — every C export exists in the Rust `.so`, matching name, `FUNC` type, and `GLOBAL DEFAULT` binding.\n\n## Behavioral equivalence\n\nRather than eyeballing it, I differentially tested both `.so`s side by side via `dlopen`:\n\n- **71,210 value cases**, zero mismatches: pseudo-random inputs at every length 0–300 × 3 buffer sizes, plus **exhaustive** coverage of all 256 single-byte and all 65,536 two-byte inputs. Each case compared the full buffer *including 32 untouched tail bytes* (to catch over-writes) and asserted the return pointer identity.\n- **12 abort/validation cases**, zero mismatches: both libraries `SIGABRT` on precisely the same conditions, including the `hex_maxlen == bin_len*2` boundary, the `bin_len == SIZE_MAX/2` boundary, and the `maxlen=0, binlen=0` case.\n\n## Translation notes\n\nThe tricky part was the branchless hex-digit expression, which relies on C's implicit promotions:\n\n```c\nx = (unsigned char)(87U + c + (((c - 10U) >> 8) & ~38U)) << 8 | ...\n```\n\nBecause of the `10U` literal, `c - 10U` is **unsigned** arithmetic — for digits 0–9 it wraps to a huge value whose logical `>> 8` and mask produce a bias that lands on `'0'+c` after the truncating `unsigned char` cast. I reproduced this with explicit `wrapping_sub`/`wrapping_add` on `u32` plus `as u8`, instead of \"cleaning it up\" into an `if c < 10` branch, so the modulo-2³² semantics stay exact. I also preserved the deliberately inverted byte packing (low nibble in x's *high* byte).\n\nTwo faithfulness details worth flagging, both intentional and **not** fixed per the instructions:\n\n- The C source hardcodes `18446744073709551615UL` instead of `SIZE_MAX`, so the guard is only correct on LP64 targets. I kept the literal and documented it rather than substituting `usize::MAX`.\n- Validation is `bin_len >= SIZE_MAX/2 || hex_maxlen <= bin_len * 2U`, evaluated in that short-circuit order before any store. Note the guard checks lengths but never `hex`/`bin` for `NULL` — hence the matching `SIGSEGV` in two test cases above. That's the original contract, reproduced as-is.\n\nOne choice I should call out for your review: I set `panic = \"abort\"` in the release profile. Unwinding out of an `extern \"C\"` function is UB, and the only failure path here is a deliberate `abort()`, so this is safe and matches C behavior — but if you intend to link this crate into a host that expects unwinding, that setting is worth revisiting."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1060,"cache_read_input_tokens":24599,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1060,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c912144-fe92-4148-9b42-37e5922e7e32","uuid":"740a4065-edc9-4a3d-aad7-992f30cc0824","timestamp":"2026-08-18T15:03:36.869Z"}
